Additional context
After finding this I dug (very) deep and noticed, that C++ exceptions in general don't work anymore. E.g. I added the following code to the top of torch/csrc/autograd/engine.cpp which will be executed very early:
int make_foo(){
    try{
        throw std::runtime_error("foo");
    }catch(...){}
    return 42;
}
static int foo = make_foo();

And after a mere python -c 'import torch' the app crashes with the same terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::runtime_error'. This fully reproduces every time with the self-compiled version, but not with the PyPi version. However that is not built with CUDA 11 and hence does not support our A100 GPUs
Using git bisect I found e34992ebee to be the first failing commit. Checking this it seems that disabling Kineto in CMake avoids the issue.
However I'm very puzzled how merely using Kineto can completely break the C++ exception handling.
Reducing this further I got this minimal example:
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cupti.h>
void foo(){
  CUpti_SubscriberHandle subscriber;
  cuptiSubscribe(&subscriber, (CUpti_CallbackFunc) nullptr, nullptr);
}
int main(){
  try{
    throw std::runtime_error("Hello");
  }catch(...){
  }
}

Compiled with g++ testRaise.cpp -l/sw/installed/CUDAcore/11.1.1/extras/CUPTI/lib64/libcupti_static.a -ldl -lpthread -lrt and running makes it crash using a recentish g++. With the system GCC 4.8 it works.
--> I think linking the static CUPTI is not a good idea in general. Using the shared lib works.
